\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}


\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}
\label{sec:nli_validation}

\paragraph{False negative rate.}
Validating all 100 NLI decisions against an LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) yields 91/100 agreement (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\kappa = 0.43$, depressed by prevalence skew \citep{feinstein1990kappa}), with FNR $= 8.4\%$ [95\% Wilson CI: 3.7\%, 15.9\%] (8 of 12 rejected pairs judged content-preserving).
Since this annotator shares the rewriter model, the FNR may be optimistic; GPT-4o cross-validation below provides model-independent verification.
Three robustness checks confirm these do not compromise conclusions: BT stable across 11 NLI thresholds (${\leq}3$pp variation; \S\ref{sec:nli_threshold}), no covariate differences between retained and discarded pairs ($p > 0.12$; \S\ref{sec:consistency}), and no directional bias in false negatives (Fisher $p = 0.35$).
The gate operates as a precision-oriented filter (precision $= 98.9\%$); verbosity-rejected pairs have larger edit distances (Cliff's $\delta = +0.45$, $p < 0.001$), making estimates lower bounds.
Full-scale GPT-4o validation on all 200 decisions yields 92.1\% formality / 82.6\% verbosity pass precision, the lower verbosity figure reflecting direction-aware gating permissiveness (17.4\% vs.\ 7.9\% FPR).
Admitted false positives would inflate verbosity BT only if content-leaked pairs systematically favor the styled variant; the GEE originality OR ${<}1$ indicates the opposite direction, making the estimate a conservative lower bound.

\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}
\label{sec:nli_threshold}
A sweep across 11 NLI thresholds (0.50--0.98) confirms robustness: all 44 judge$\times$axis$\times$threshold combinations are significant (${\leq}3$pp variation; Appendix~\ref{app:nli_sweep}), and the bimodal NLI score distribution places the 0.90 threshold on a plateau.


\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\ Pairwise}
\label{sec:likert_pairwise}

\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}
Applying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\% formal preference ($p = 0.0004$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).
This is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments---one detects bias, the other does not.
A $4\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power: the Likert effect size shrinks to $d = {-}0.025$ (near zero) while BT remains stable at $67.5\%$ ($p < 0.0001$)---if the gap were merely statistical power, $d$ would remain non-zero at larger $n$.
Bootstrap subsampling confirms: $d$ remains flat near zero ($d_{\text{median}} = {-}0.017$) as CIs narrow from ${\pm}0.3$ to ${\pm}0.1$, never excluding zero (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_attenuation}).
Attenuation follows a severity spectrum: at moderate bias (BT 63--67\%), it is complete ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$); at stronger effects (BT ${>}70\%$), Likert partially detects but attenuates ${>}60\%$ (MT-Bench $d \approx 0.15$ for BT ${\sim}70\%$)---explaining why three of eleven bias-positive cells escape the Blindspot (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, $\ddagger$ rows).

The frameworks are complementary: RATE measures aggregate quality via Likert ATE; CSD targets per-axis style preference via forced-choice comparison, eliminating ties.
The Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\% across all nine configurations; a 100-point continuous scale does not resolve this ($p = 0.725$/$0.117$; Appendix~\ref{app:likert100}), confirming a paradigm difference rather than granularity limitation.


\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}
To confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity}).
Both pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).
The same attenuation pattern replicates on verbosity (Appendix~\ref{app:verb_sensitivity}), where all six Likert methods remain non-significant despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\%, $p < 0.001$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy: seven measurement methods applied to the same formality style-transformed data. Only pairwise methods (top two rows) reach significance. All Likert-based methods fail.}
\label{tab:sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & Statistic & $p$ & Data \\
\midrule
BT model & 63.0\% $[55.7, 70.3]$ & $0.0004$ & All 173 comp. \\
Sign test (filtered) & 73.9\% (34/46) & 0.006 & Consistent \\
\midrule
Likert ATE ($t$-test) & $d = 0.102$ & 0.332 & NLI$\geq$0.90 \\
Binarized Likert & 60.6\% & 0.296 & Non-tied \\
Ordinal reg.\ (prop.\ odds) & $\beta = {-}0.079$ & 0.769 & NLI$\geq$0.90 \\
GEE (exchangeable) & $\beta = {-}0.084$ & 0.587 & NLI$\geq$0.90 \\
Wilcoxon signed-rank & $W = 274$ & 0.905 & NLI$\geq$0.90 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability at observed effect magnitudes (not post-hoc power; cf.\ \citealt{hoenig2001abuse}): across six cells, Likert requires $4$--$206\times$ the current $n$ ($336$--$27{,}159$ pairs; Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}) to detect effects BT identifies at $p < 0.001$ with $n \leq 100$.
\paragraph{Measurement attenuation vs.\ construct divergence.}
An alternative interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure different constructs---absolute quality vs.\ ordinal preference---making the divergence a construct difference rather than measurement failure.
\citet{tripathi2025pairwise} independently confirm that pairwise and pointwise protocols exhibit distinct sensitivity profiles, with pairwise preferences being more volatile---consistent with this construct divergence interpretation.
Both interpretations lead to the same conclusion: Likert audits miss style preferences that drive pairwise-based alignment pipelines (RLHF, DPO).

\paragraph{Detection vs.\ amplification.}
\label{sec:amplification}
\citet{jeong2025comparative} show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases (the Comparative Trap).
Format-level amplification cannot be fully excluded, but six independent controls bound this risk: (i)~NLI gate ablation reverses NLI-fail pairs to BT 41.7\% (Appendix~\ref{app:nli_ablation}); (ii)~Claude Sonnet 4 shows no bias with the identical pairwise format (BT 56.2\%/54.1\%, n.s.); (iii)~cross-axis BT variation (register 55\% $<$ formality 67\% $<$ verbosity 77\%) is inconsistent with uniform format amplification; (iv)~rewriting artifact absorbed by GEE decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:artifact_control}); (v)~debiased prompting reduces bias with axis-dependent effectiveness (verbosity $-13.1$pp; formality $-5.7$/$-0.4$pp), suggesting varying structural depth; (vi)~four independent architectures all confirm the Blindspot with consistent GEE confound structure (\S\ref{sec:cross_judge}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}
\caption{Detection probability at observed effect magnitudes. 80\% power crossings: sign test $n \approx 62$, BT $n \approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \approx 1{,}473$. At typical scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), pairwise methods reach 80\% power while Likert methods remain far below. At $n = 355$, Likert $d = {-}0.025$ confirms the gap is structural, not merely a power issue.}
\label{fig:power_curve}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication}
\label{sec:cross_judge}

Three independent judges replicate the Blindspot: Qwen3-32B (BT 66.5\%, $p < 0.0001$; Likert $p = 0.908$), Llama-3.3-70B (BT 69.5\%, $p = 0.0001$; Likert $p = 0.846$), and Gemini-2.5-Flash (confirmatory: BT 70.8\%, $p < 0.0001$; Likert $d = 0.05$, n.s.).
Gemini's formality confound is originality-dominated (Style OR $= 1.64$, n.s.; Orig OR $= 2.51$, $p = 0.002$), unlike the other three judges (\S\ref{sec:direction}).

\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}
\label{sec:cross_axis}

Verbosity provides the strongest Blindspot: all four target judges show consistent preference (GPT-4o 76.0\%, Qwen3-32B 77.3\%, Llama-70B 73.5\%, Gemini 70.8\%; all $p \leq 0.0001$), while Likert ATE remains non-significant for all ($p = 0.179$--$0.752$).
GEE confirms massive style effects (OR $8.2$--$13.1$, all $p < .0001$) with originality ORs below 1 ($0.38$--$0.49$; only Llama-70B significant at $p = .020$, yet Style OR exceeds Orig OR by $22\times$), confirming style dominance (Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
Both rewriting directions favor verbose responses (90.7\%/80.6\%); controlling for length leaves the style OR unchanged ($<$2\%).
Evaluator-producer overlap applies only to Qwen3-32B; an independent GPT-4o rewriter bounds this to a $22\%$ Style OR reduction ($13.10 \to 10.17$; Appendix~\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).

\subsection{Register: Judge-Dependent Confound Structure}
\label{sec:negative_control}

Register is exploratory and judge-dependent (Llama-70B BT 63.5\%, GPT-4o 57.0\%, Qwen3-32B n.s.; Table~\ref{tab:gee}).

\subsection{GEE Conditional Decomposition and Confound Structure}
\label{sec:direction}

GEE conditional decomposition \citep{liang1986gee} with exchangeable working correlation and robust standard errors separates style from originality effects (Appendix~\ref{app:direction}; model diagnostics including correlation structure robustness and interaction tests in Appendix~\ref{app:gee_diagnostics}).
After controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant (GPT-4o: OR $= 2.58$, $p = 0.002$; Qwen3-32B: OR $= 2.15$, $p < 0.0001$, $n = 355$; Llama-70B: OR $= 4.81$, $p < 0.0001$; Table~\ref{tab:gee}); the significant originality ORs confirm residual confounding.
The $4\times$ replication ($n = 355$) confirms the Blindspot: BT $67.5\%$ (halved CI), Likert n.s.\ ($p = 0.60$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{GEE conditional decomposition (exchangeable correlation) of style and originality effects. Verbosity is style-dominated across all four target judges; formality shows varying confound structure.}
\label{tab:gee}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llclc@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
Config & OR & $p$ & OR & $p$ \\
\midrule
Qwen3 verb.\ & 13.10 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 & .096 \\
GPT-4o verb.\ & 11.34 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.45 & .062 \\
Llama-70B verb.\ & 8.21 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.38 & .020 \\
Gemini verb.\ & 8.98 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.46 & .061 \\
\midrule
GPT-4o form.\ & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\
Qwen3 form.\ & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & .0001 \\
Qwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 & ${<}.0001$ & 4.99 & ${<}.0001$ \\
Llama-70B form.\ & 4.81 & ${<}.0001$ & 3.12 & .0007 \\
Gemini form.\ & 1.64 & .101 & 2.51 & .002 \\
\midrule
GPT-4o reg.\ & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\
Qwen3 reg.\ & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & ${<}.0001$ \\
Llama-70B reg.\ & 3.33 & .0003 & 3.07 & .0008 \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$. 95\% CIs in Appendix.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The confound structure is consistent across all four target judges for verbosity (style-dominated: Style OR $8.2$--$13.1$, Orig OR $0.38$--$0.49$); formality is conditionally style-driven in three judges but originality-dominated in Gemini; register is judge-dependent.
Verbosity Style ORs of $8$--$13$ are comparable to the strongest epidemiological effect sizes, indicating a dominant driver of pairwise outcomes rather than a subtle confound.
Claude Sonnet 4 serves as negative control (BT 56.2\%/54.1\%, n.s.; TOST equivalence confirmed at $\Delta = 15$pp, near the minimum margin achievable at $n = 87$ with 80\% power ($\Delta_{\min} = 15.7$pp); a tighter margin requires larger $n$; Appendix~\ref{app:claude_tost}).
The Blindspot manifests in eight of eleven bias-positive cells; three exceptions (BT ${>}69\%$, $d \geq 0.14$) confirm it is effect-size dependent.
Same-style double-rewrite controls confirm a rewriting artifact (Appendix~\ref{app:artifact_control}), absorbed by GEE's originality term.

